Tactical treatment on the generated map, in our own CSS and SVG

Coordinate grid, layered glowing coastline, marker glow via an SVG filter, and
crosshair ticks on the two sites actually above the closure trigger. Not on the
"not present" sites: decoration must not claim attention the reading does not
support.

All self-contained geometry, no background-image and no external reference,
because this markup is committed into the recorded run and re-rendered through
the page sanitizer, which strips use, image, foreignObject, script and anchor
tags. A test asserts the filter survives that round trip and that none of the
stripped elements are used.

WHAT WAS SUGGESTED AND NOT TAKEN, with reasons:

  3D globe. Agreed it is wrong: a global projection for a dataset living
  between Tampa Bay and the Keys is geographically wrong, and Three.js is an
  external script the CSP blocks by design.

  Tailwind classes (border-cyan-500/30, bg-emerald-500 shadow-[...],
  bg-[radial-gradient(...)], animate-ping). Fourth pass of the same error.
  There is no Tailwind in this project; those resolve to nothing.

  "We need to introduce a playhead sweep matching the trace duration" and
  "add animated radar rings over the map". Both already shipped. The suggestion
  was made from static screenshots, so nothing that moves was visible.

AND ONE CLAIM TO IGNORE OUTRIGHT: a suggestion argued the hydrophone must stay
or the entry loses eligibility for a "Best Multimodal UX ($5,000)" category.
That category came from the same source earlier in this project and was
recorded as UNVERIFIED at the time; the tracks actually read off the rules page
were Fortified Enterprise Fleet ($20,000) and Individual/Hobbyist (2 x $10,000).
A claim repeated back to you is still unverified. Re-read the rules page before
choosing a submission category.

The hydrophone stays for the honest reason: it demonstrates real multimodal
capability on real data. It is not the most important feature, and it does not
feed the verdict.

94 tests pass.
